th infinitely many nonzero coefficients."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5e46b2e9e0068516c66fd942","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_polynomial_d48aa96b_curated20261026_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the degree of $P(x) = 0x^5+2x^3+1$?","options":["$5$.","$2$.","$3$.","$0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"01d174c617a0d2687fb3b2bf","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_polynomial_d48aa96b_curated20261026_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which displayed function is defined and continuous for every real input?","options":["$f(x) = \\frac{1}{x}$.","$P(x) = x^4-2x^2+1$.","$g(x) = \\sqrt{x}$.","$h(x) = x^{-1}+x$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6d17358a1b15a0366dc55df5","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_polynomial_d48aa96b_curated20261026_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Using factorisation, what are the zeros of $P(x) = x^2-3x+2$?","options":["$0$ and $3$.","$1$ and $2$.","$-1$ and $-2$.","$2$ and $3$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e7d91ef237098fd4aff5c272","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_polynomial_d48aa96b_curated20261026_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which expression is a polynomial of degree $0$?","options":["$P(x) = 5x$.","$P(x) = 5$.","$P(x) = x^5$.","$P(x) = \\frac{5}{x}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d1b0c2a6e28de47f11fa2e76","time_limit":20},{"id":"polynomial_new_curated20261026_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Under this page's convention, what are the degree and the real zeros of the zero polynomial $P(x)=0$?","options":["Degree zero; there are no real zeros.","Degree zero; every real number is a zero.","No assigned degree; there are no real zeros.","No assigned degree; every real number is a zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"eb65ba97c5084aa73fe624e6","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_polynomial_d48aa96b_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is the degree of the polynomial $P(x)=(x^2+3x)^2-x^4$?","options":["$4$","$3$","$2$","$6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"293c718eadd14ad300437bdd","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_polynomial_d48aa96b_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $P(x)=a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+a_3x^3$ with real coefficients $a_0,a_1,a_2,a_3$, and suppose $P(1)=P(2)=P(3)=P(4)=0$. What follows?","options":["$a_0=a_1=a_2=a_3=0$, so $P$ is the zero polynomial.","No such $P$ exists, because a cubic has at most $3$ zeros.","$P(x)=c(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)(x-4)$ for some real $c\\ne0$.","$P(x)=c(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)$ for some real $c\\ne0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1125507387c889b5bb67e59b","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_polynomial_d48aa96b_topup20261028_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $P(x)=-x^3+50x^2+10$ for $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Which statement about the sign of $P(x)$ is correct?","options":["$P(x)>0$ for all sufficiently large $x$, since $50$ is the largest coefficient.","$P(x)>0$ for all sufficiently large $x$, since a cubic rises on the right.","$P(x)<0$ for all sufficiently large $x$, since the term $-x^3$ outweighs the others.","$P(x)<0$ for all $x>0$, since the leading coefficient $-1$ is negative."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"972f29e8008dcfddde004c4f","time_limit":45}]}}